#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 900.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189116} :activity/id #uuid "e272f91b-9886-585a-977f-32ac3449f7d2" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049521 :topic/id #uuid "fc0b243b-d01d-5899-b73f-ccd1f75622f1" :topic/math-academy-id 3895} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189087 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189367 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ for $0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/09/09b25bca42f62b6567cecf4b14987113003ccea6dd869a569be75af4fa1781c9.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00.\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve at this point. \n\n![](images/00/001914b359c9c95f6a8fceb655a5233242065eb4231765bcfc0d652ebbf4eed8.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines become steeper and steeper as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](images/40/40de3bbd7f8422ff4ab4af6091497579f8031b8a43296e466f8c23ed4a121097.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* as we approach $C,$ it would appear that these tangent lines become vertical. \n\n![](images/ca/caf6255e2ae97865da52dec64413e2e61a9a807f50b54ae4eae2f2b96f030c30.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes vertical like this, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at $C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "69760698-2d63-55ca-aa44-895e4a5a7c66" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6185 :tutorial/title "Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases"} :step/id #uuid "c57d45c4-8a9c-599f-993b-2326fc1fb56f" :step/math-academy-id 37500 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189103}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189103 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189374 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1-\\sin\\theta$ has a vertical tangent at $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines' slopes grow to infinity as we approach $\\theta = \\dfrac \\pi 2.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{-}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty \\qquad\\textrm{and}\\qquad \\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^{+}} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\pm\\infty.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta-\\cos(2\\theta), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=\\cos\\theta - \\sin2\\theta.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta }{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\dfrac\\pi 2$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\n- Applying L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the left-sided limit, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{(\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ))^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the limit in the numerator, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})+2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =1-2 \\\\  & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor the limit in the denominator, it can be shown that\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to (\\pi/2)^-} \\left(\\cos\\theta - 2\\sin(2\\theta)\\right) = 0^-,\n$$\n\nwhere the notation $0^-$ means that the denominator approaches zero from the negative numbers.  \n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Using similar methods, it can be shown that the right-sided limit is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(2\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{-1}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, we conclude that there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta=\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}.$ \n\n![](images/ea/ea0a03e575d962bd8d69505e20166dcc60c77cfe933f30762bb81296ccf7a797.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "7b372073-8035-5855-9367-f29c35485805" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6269 :tutorial/title "Using Limits to Find Vertical Tangents"} :step/id #uuid "f33de88b-4d60-5405-829e-a88dd1826520" :step/math-academy-id 37995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189147}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189116 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189380 :tutorial/content "Let's consider the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ for $0\\leq \\theta < 2\\pi,$ shown below.\n\n![](images/e7/e72d6a7d4b490a37f99723d0cde12a378f038b43ba373547e9c8feaf71952717.png)\n\nUsing previously discussed techniques, it's straightforward to show that at point $C$ where $\\theta = \\pi,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = \\dfrac{y'(\\theta)}{x'(\\theta)} = \\dfrac00.\n$$\n\nTo see what's happening at this point, let's zoom in and observe the behavior of the curve.\n\n![](images/d0/d03ca27e8cecd32aebc0e6914ac2ab128bce57ab876b96e8aaa91b562041ba4c.png)\n\nIf we look carefully, we notice that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach the point $C$ from either direction.\n\n![](images/4f/4fda95ed912c83ac36ec772b6afa6554ad8a81b7c98a330fd774d501b4bbecdf.png)\n\nTherefore, as we take the *limit* of the tangent lines as we approach $C,$ it would appear that they become horizontal. \n\n![](images/50/50f8bd688c55d8458a9db100469cd62138efc200402496d3adb18f90ea53e747.png)\n\nWhen the limit of the tangent lines becomes horizontal like this, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at $C.$" :tutorial/id #uuid "e9679f34-9ef1-55a0-ba1a-15cccaecb4a7" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6303 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "ecb3fe0f-d538-57b4-b4e4-a660ce2892d4" :step/math-academy-id 38153 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189146}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189117 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188297 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188004 :question/id #uuid "70e3ea0e-2f39-5715-98a3-2a0e686ffa76" :question/math-academy-id "e-10116"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "1bff423b-022b-5914-a134-c4c346e91b8f" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048273} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049927} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186051036}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188551} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188566} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549000} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549007} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549008} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549021} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569981} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570016} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570042}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Horizontal Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"} :step/id #uuid "5c1c429e-ced3-5909-92a2-519ae11a4322" :step/math-academy-id 38154 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189087}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189146 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189391 :tutorial/content "So, it seems that the curve $r = 1+\\cos\\theta$ has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$ However, to be sure of this, we need to check that the tangent lines flatten out as we approach $\\theta = \\pi.$ In other words, we need to check that\n\n \n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0.\n$$\n\nLet's now verify that this is true:\n\nUsing techniques discussed in previous lessons, it's straightforward to show that\n\n$$\nx'(\\theta)=-\\sin\\theta(2\\cos\\theta+1), \\qquad \ny'(\\theta)=2\\cos^2\\theta +\\cos\\theta - 1.\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{-\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta (2\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +1)}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{2\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe already know that substituting $\\theta = \\pi$ gives the indeterminate form $\\dfrac00,$ so evaluating this limit by direct substitution won't work. However, we can evaluate this limit using L'Hopital's rule.\n\nApplying L'Hopital's rule, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1}{\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{(2{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta -1)^{′}}{(\\operatorname{sin}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta )^{′}}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{-4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta -\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi }(\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\theta +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi +\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\pi }{2\\operatorname{cos}⁡2\\pi +\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi } \\\\  & =\\frac{4\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (0)+0}{2\\cdot 1+(-1)} \\\\  & =\\frac{0}{1} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, since\n\n$$\n\\lim\\limits_{\\theta \\to \\pi} \\dfrac{\\textrm d y}{\\textrm d x} = 0\n$$\n\nwe conclude that our curve does indeed have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta = \\pi.$\n\n![](images/f1/f172dbe1e7fa97fa924feed4ee193a620b1224a985a4e406ef4e46afbdc0cb2a.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "6a54c219-b6df-5e73-8673-14f48b1d69af" :tutorial/math-academy-id 6384 :tutorial/title "Using Limits to Find Horizontal Tangents"} :step/id #uuid "8f383aba-74ba-5583-9105-a9ee0b48e2fd" :step/math-academy-id 38606 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189117}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186189147 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188316 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188023 :question/id #uuid "2175ea86-60e7-5402-810d-1372b455c28d" :question/math-academy-id "e-10244"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "25fd0bb9-4a47-5f76-bdba-cdcd19cf141f" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188567} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188613} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548978} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186548979} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549025} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186549031} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569964}] :knowledge-point/title "Finding Vertical Tangents in Non-Differentiable Cases"} :step/id #uuid "9390067a-786b-5a47-889a-88feebe31790" :step/math-academy-id 38614}] :activity/title "Horizontal and Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186188485}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046673} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570055} :activity/id #uuid "3c665c19-95e5-52b1-a4a9-44e5ed397b25" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049521 :topic/id #uuid "fc0b243b-d01d-5899-b73f-ccd1f75622f1" :topic/math-academy-id 3895} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570054 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186188613 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8fc6cbcc-05a7-5766-bdee-b250f004309b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185959} {:answer/id #uuid "c87ca7e2-0411-592b-abbd-eafcf123e695" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185960} {:answer/id #uuid "ef9e88db-9d54-57b4-bb4f-dcb5bd723a62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185961} {:answer/id #uuid "fedabed4-adb9-5f87-9809-9dc356d6d712" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185962} {:answer/id #uuid "51ddbbea-78d7-5b8c-85dd-ea5ffe88e196" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185963}] :answer-field/id #uuid "7574cf83-3cd6-5a8f-a652-3099a4de71c9" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186185958}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486} :question/id #uuid "8c055904-2b51-5183-9e8c-f8a23f95c6d4" :question/math-academy-id "q-145085" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r = θ^{2}\\cos θ$ for $θ ∈ (- π, π)$. Given that\n\n$x^{′}(θ) = 2θ\\cos^{2} (θ) - θ^{2}\\sin (2θ), y^{′}(θ) = θ\\sin (2θ) + θ^{2}\\cos (2θ)$,\nand $x^{′}(0) = 0$, which of the following statements are true?\n\n1. $y^{′}(0) = 0$\n2. $lim_(θ → 0^{-})\\frac{dy}{dx} = ∞$\n3. The curve has a vertical tangent at $θ = 0$\n\n> A scientific calculator is required to answer this question." :question/requires-calculator true} :step/id #uuid "4222932c-dad4-5f43-9169-5224c25483a0"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570055 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570042 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "313ec59c-5153-5bc7-8ccf-d7d49fff1f8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569515} {:answer/id #uuid "5ffa960b-f359-54f3-bfe0-2df8dcedbd09" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569589} {:answer/id #uuid "70b0c1ec-d41a-509f-b845-f8b330322fdc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569627} {:answer/id #uuid "b3ef2881-d27a-5bf5-a98e-41fea5f9b27b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569729} {:answer/id #uuid "e136fdfa-26d6-5d9d-8c09-dcdc748b9749" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569801}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569801} :answer-field/id #uuid "e16e2010-e951-50c2-8dc9-1ddcbae83f31" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569936}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "fcf32a74-971a-505b-b459-ef18eedf0a90" :question/math-academy-id "q-143715" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})({cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\cancel{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\theta )}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/f4/f4609e6433a861ef255cbeeac60c8a316e5c14a89df45030e26e526e3a1253a4.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "48a6f89e-446f-5ff8-9fe2-e64eff3be6f6" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570062}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570057 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569964 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "70f01508-769b-5ace-9917-5f7722cd7ce8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569628} {:answer/id #uuid "846fb0c9-3ec1-54f5-b33c-0e023a6c3914" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569662} {:answer/id #uuid "864177d3-700a-5680-a7ae-fe5e85113f8a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569665} {:answer/id #uuid "c63e937b-281c-584f-875d-739cf8a9c9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569758} {:answer/id #uuid "f0214696-0b2a-5443-87e4-6a0873c5d70e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569822}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569662} :answer-field/id #uuid "fce87f54-6287-5426-bb0b-5253c4ba2656" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569953}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "1355c292-a399-578d-8bac-2939b3befda0" :question/math-academy-id "q-145039" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r={cos}^{2}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta ,\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=-\\infty$\n\nIII. The curve has a vertical tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :question/worked-solution "For a given polar curve, there is a vertical tangent at $\\theta ={\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}\n$$\n\nis infinite. This means that\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a vertical tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty \\,\\text{and}\\,{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\pm \\infty .\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${x}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =-3\\cdot 1\\cdot 0 \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to {(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}^{+},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{\\cancel{cos}\\theta ({cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta )}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta }{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta }) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{+}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{+}} \\\\  & =-\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is true. Using the calculations from part II, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to (\\pi /2)^{-}}(\\frac{{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2{sin}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}{-3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})}) \\\\  & =\\frac{0-2\\cdot 1}{-3\\cdot 1\\cdot {0}^{+}} \\\\  & =\\frac{-2}{{0}^{-}} \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSince the limits of our derivative are both infinite, there is a vertical tangent to the curve at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$\n\n![](images/50/50637a502a56fc242ba5d8e2fda59ddc899295d4b14f7ae692869536b95ef661.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\""} :step/id #uuid "4b0acf10-61d4-57d2-8625-d0ac303e7832" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570054}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570062 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570016 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "742dc51a-8d96-560e-94d1-367169dd0392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569634} {:answer/id #uuid "c42187b6-4c29-5b59-b6f3-624ff226be39" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569750} {:answer/id #uuid "d8c3238c-1f70-547d-9e2f-c99ec90befda" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569784} {:answer/id #uuid "e1476574-57fd-5884-b17f-d8c6e1916591" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569802} {:answer/id #uuid "fb85d25b-c824-57bd-a2e9-670821d95958" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569839}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569634} :answer-field/id #uuid "4b4c2f95-49e5-5e7b-9fbf-f50c23d0825b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569873}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "a72cce3a-417b-593f-a3ca-475feb70703d" :question/math-academy-id "q-144906" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=4{sin}^{2}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )=8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta ),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})$ is undefined\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2}) \\\\  & =3\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\pi )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi ) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{3{sin}^{2}⁡(2\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{12{sin}^{2}⁡(\\theta ){cos}^{2}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{12{sin}^{\\cancel{2}}⁡(\\theta ){\\cancel{cos}}^{2}(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\cancel{sin}\\theta {cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\theta )\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\theta )}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(3{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})\\operatorname{cos}⁡(2\\cdot \\frac{\\pi }{2})}{8\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(3{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{12\\cdot 1\\cdot (-1)}{0\\cdot (3\\cdot 0-2)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-12}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/32/32a586a1fe160a6c3a2cfee3cfb78af3d596679cf40abedeb0b8d622a3cf736c.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None of the statements are true.\""} :step/id #uuid "5e976fb1-d35b-5c20-ade1-2919ee77f71b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570065}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570065 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569981 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0fe0a563-e910-5a09-9b65-423c48c76310" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569468} {:answer/id #uuid "4ede0597-e201-5e81-953f-0df38ea579f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569556} {:answer/id #uuid "7a918a37-015b-582f-9559-4c91adb530ad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569645} {:answer/id #uuid "a2b00882-095d-51f2-92e9-ed2560cdc8b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569701} {:answer/id #uuid "e7f0df0d-c797-5e92-9ea6-b642a07c5dd9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499} :answer/value "None of the statements are true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569813}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186569556} :answer-field/id #uuid "ca41ebba-af00-5d55-bf09-af4bba36416b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569926}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "3f8bf9ef-a3d1-5fa9-97cc-d10fddcf1cd1" :question/math-academy-id "q-144911" :question/problem "Consider the polar curve $r=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{3}⁡\\theta$ for $\\theta \\in [0,\\pi ).$ Given that \n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(\\theta )={cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4),\\,{y}^{′}(\\theta )=\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta (5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3),\n$$\n\nand ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. ${y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0$\n\nII. ${lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0$\n\nIII. The curve has a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$\n\n*Hint: It is not necessary to use L'Hopital's rule to evaluate the limit in part II.*" :question/worked-solution "When a polar curve is differentiable at a point ${\\theta }_{0}$, there is a horizontal tangent at ${\\theta }_{0}$ if\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}{{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})}=0\n$$\n\nwhich is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{y}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})=0\\,\\text{and}\\,{x}^{′}({\\theta }_{0})\\ne 0.\n$$\n\nHowever, if the curve is continuous at ${\\theta }_{0}$ yet the derivative ${y}^{′}(x)$ takes the indeterminate form $\\frac{0}{0}$ at this point, we say that the curve has a horizontal tangent at this point if\n\n$$\n{lim}_{\\theta \\to {\\theta }_{0}}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=0.\n$$\n\nIn this case, we're told that ${x}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2})=0.$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is true. Substituting $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ into the expression for ${y}^{′}(\\theta ),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{y}^{′}(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}){cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})(5{cos}^{2}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2})-3) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3) \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement II is false. Computing the limit as $\\theta \\to \\frac{\\pi }{2},$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x} & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}\\frac{{y}^{′}(\\theta )}{{x}^{′}(\\theta )} \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {cos}^{2}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{3}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta {\\cancel{cos}}^{2}\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{{cos}^{\\cancel{3}}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & ={lim}_{\\theta \\to \\pi /2}(\\frac{\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -3)}{\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta \\,(5{cos}^{2}⁡\\theta -4)}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-3)}{0\\cdot (5\\cdot 0-4)} \\\\  & =\\frac{-3}{0} \\\\  & \\ne 0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Statement III is false. Since the limit in part II does *not* equal to $0,$ there curve does *not* have a horizontal tangent at $\\theta =\\frac{\\pi }{2}.$ In fact, it is a vertical tangent.\n\n![](images/d7/d7881ba860bcc32301a162eb200ea9075b06b63c780be74c14aedbcb1654eaaa.png)\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\""} :step/id #uuid "63ba05e5-32e7-552d-a5e3-1d3bf124648b" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570057}}] :activity/title "Horizontal and Vertical Tangents to Polar Curves in Non-Differentiable Cases" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186569437}]}
